function</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">s: X \to P.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">\rho\circ s: X \to C.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">2) Equivalence (up to renaming)</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Theorem (Labeling–Sorting Equivalence).</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">For any labeling  there exist  such that .
Conversely, any sorting triple  determines a labeling . Two such realizations are equivalent up to relabeling of bins.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Constructive proof (→).
Given , take  a disjoint union of nonempty bin-sets indexed by . Pick any section  with . Define</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">s(x)= e(\lambda(x)).</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Constructive proof (←).
Given , set . The induced equivalence relation is</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">x\sim y \iff \rho(s(x))=\rho(s(y)),</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Corollary. Labeling and sorting induce the same partition of  (same kernel pair) and hence the same quotient set . They are two presentations of the same structure:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">\text{(names on items)} \;\equiv\; \text{(places for items)}.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">3) Categorical view (optional but tight)</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Treat  and  as discrete categories. A labeling is a functor .
A sorting is a factorization .
Two factorizations with the same composite are equivalent in the slice category . So “labels vs. locations” are isomorphic objects over .</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">4) Information-theoretic note</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Labeling/sorting both compress description length by replacing  item-level recall with  class-level recall. The entropy of the class variable  is</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">H(Y) = -\sum_{c\in C} p(c)\log p(c),</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">5) Nonverbal competence ⇒ verbal tests can be flawed</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Because labeling and sorting are equivalent as partitions, a nonverbal human who cannot produce or comprehend linguistic labels can still realize  by a sorting . That is, they can:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">compute a partition  over ,</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">manifest it physically by grouping items into bins/areas.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">So written/oral tests that demand linguistic labels risk false negatives: they measure language, not the underlying classification ability (the partition). A fair test can therefore be:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Nonverbal sorting task. Present objects  and bins with shapes/colors (or even unlabeled areas). Ask the subject to “put together things that go together.” Any coherent partition  demonstrates the same cognitive act as labeling—without words.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">6) Extensions (brief)</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Multi-label / overlapping classes: use  (subsets of ); sorting becomes placing items in multiple overlapping regions (Venn areas / stacked bins).</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Hierarchies: let  be a tree/poset; sorting into nested containers corresponds to labeling with respect to a refinement chain of partitions.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Spatial constraints: if positions lie in a metric space , sorting is  plus a classifier  with . Conversely, embed  into  and place items at those embeddings.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Bottom line: labeling and sorting are the same mathematical operation—partitioning—realized either symbolically (names) or physically (locations). Hence, nonverbal sorting robustly evidences the very capacity many verbal tests intend (but often fail) to measure.</p></div></div><div class="absolute bottom-0 right-2 pointer-events-none" style="transform: none;"><div class="rounded-lg transition min-w-max pointer-events-auto translate-y-4 bg-bg-100/80 backdrop-blur-sm translate-x-1 group-hover:translate-x-0.5 border-0.5 border-border-300 p-0.5 shadow-sm opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><button class="inline-flex
