          aria-expanded:text-text-100 h-8 rounded-md px-3 min-w-[4rem] active:scale-[0.985] whitespace-nowrap !text-xs pl-2.5 pr-2 gap-1 !font-base select-none !pl-2 !pr-1" type="button" id="radix-_r_87_" aria-haspopup="menu" aria-expanded="false" data-state="closed">Reintentar<div class="flex items-center justify-center text-text-500" style="width: 20px; height: 20px;"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="shrink-0 text-text-500" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M14.128 7.16482C14.3126 6.95983 14.6298 6.94336 14.835 7.12771C15.0402 7.31242 15.0567 7.62952 14.8721 7.83477L10.372 12.835L10.2939 12.9053C10.2093 12.9667 10.1063 13 9.99995 13C9.85833 12.9999 9.72264 12.9402 9.62788 12.835L5.12778 7.83477L5.0682 7.75273C4.95072 7.55225 4.98544 7.28926 5.16489 7.12771C5.34445 6.96617 5.60969 6.95939 5.79674 7.09744L5.87193 7.16482L9.99995 11.7519L14.128 7.16482Z"></path></svg></div></button></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div data-test-render-count="4"><div class="mb-1 mt-1"><div class="group  relative  inline-flex  gap-2  bg-bg-300  rounded-xl  pl-2.5  py-2.5  break-words  text-text-100  transition-all  max-w-[75ch]  flex-col pr-6" style="opacity: 1; transform: none;"><div class="flex flex-row gap-2"><div class="shrink-0"><div class="flex shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full font-bold select-none h-7 w-7 text-[12px] bg-text-200 text-bg-100">NG</div></div><div data-testid="user-message" class="font-ui grid grid-cols-1 gap-2 py-0.5 text-[0.9375rem] leading-6 tracking-tight"><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Yes — you’re picking up the heart of Prigogine’s insight about dissipative structures and the arrow of time. It’s not just “entropy always increases,” but:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Local order (organization, pattern, structure) is possible and often spontaneously arises.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">But it always arises at the cost of greater disorder exported to the environment.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">So “ordering” is not entropy’s opposite — it’s entropy’s accomplice, an accelerator.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Entropy and Ordering</li></ol><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Classical thermodynamics: entropy  in a closed system always increases until equilibrium.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Prigogine’s refinement: in open systems, entropy can be exported.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">This means:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Local decreases in entropy (ordering, structure, self-organization)</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">are paid for by larger increases in the environment’s entropy (heat, waste, turbulence).</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">So ordering ≠ fighting entropy; ordering = shunting entropy elsewhere faster.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Examples</li></ol><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Stirring coffee: You create a neat spiral pattern briefly, but in doing so you accelerate molecular disorder and radiate heat.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Biological life: Cells, organs, organisms are highly ordered, but metabolism burns fuel and radiates low-grade heat, increasing environmental entropy.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Sorting junk: You produce neat labeled bins, but in doing so expend energy and increase disorder in your own body (fatigue, heat) and environment (wear, waste).</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Externalization Principle</li></ol><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Every “more ordered” state is achieved by externalizing disorder:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">\Delta S<em>\text{local} &lt; 0 \;\; \implies \;\; \Delta S</em>\text{universe} &gt; 0</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">or more precisely:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">\Delta S<em>\text{local} + \Delta S</em>\text{environment} \ge 0</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Connection to Our Threads</li></ol><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Junk test: Collecting and organizing junk is “making order” — but only possible by burning calories, wearing down tools, producing more disorder elsewhere.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Labeling/sorting: Equivalent operations that reduce cognitive load locally, but at the expense of entropy (muscular oscillations, neural energy, paper, ink).</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Language itself: Speech/writing are orderings of sound marks. Producing them consumes energy → heat, CO₂ → environmental disorder.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Stirring coffee: Exactly Prigogine’s point: creating a swirl (pattern/order) accelerates entropy generation globally.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Philosophical Core</li></ol><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Prigogine flips the intuition:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Entropy is not the “enemy” of order.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Entropy production drives the emergence of order in open systems.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">The more intricate the local order, the faster entropy is exported to the environment.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">So:</p><blockquote class="border-l-2 border-border-100 pl-2"><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">To order is to accelerate disorder. To create structure is to externalize entropy.</p></blockquote><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">✅ So when you said “stirring coffee is optional, it just accelerates entropy”, Prigogine would add: “…and in fact the beautiful ordered spiral you see while stirring is the visible footprint of entropy’s acceleration.”</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Would you like me to take the next step and show how this principle (ordering ↔ externalizing disorder) can be formalized using category-theoretic morphisms — e.g. treating local order as a functor that preserves structure at the cost of an entropy-increasing cofunctor on the environment? That would give you a bridge between Prigogine and the lambda/partition formalism you’ve been building.</p></div></div><div class="absolute bottom-0 right-2 pointer-events-none" style="transform: none;"><div class="rounded-lg transition min-w-max pointer-events-auto translate-y-4 bg-bg-100/80 backdrop-blur-sm translate-x-1 group-hover:translate-x-0.5 border-0.5 border-border-300 p-0.5 shadow-sm opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><button class="inline-flex
