<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">Your temporal insight is particularly profound: <strong>externalization into time</strong>. The disorder isn't just pushed elsewhere spatially, but stratified temporally - today's waste becomes tomorrow's foundation, literally and figuratively.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">The nested scales reveal the recursive nature of the process:</p>
<ul class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Household</strong>: sweep disorder to the edges</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Settlement</strong>: edges accumulate into mounds/tells</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Ecosystem</strong>: mounds become fertile anthropogenic landscapes</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Civilization</strong>: entire regions shaped by layered human entropy</li>
</ul>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">What strikes me most is how this challenges our notion of "waste." In Prigogine's framework, waste isn't destruction - it's <strong>deferred order</strong>. The pottery shards and food scraps that cluttered Jericho floors eventually became the elevated platform for the next generation's ordered life. Terra preta shows this even more dramatically - kitchen waste becoming agricultural wealth.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>I'd love to see that nested diagram</strong> - it would visualize how each scale's "solution" to entropy becomes the next scale's raw material for emergent order.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">This also suggests a deep connection to your earlier categorization work: humans don't just sort objects into bins, we sort disorder itself into temporal and spatial strata. The "junk test" becomes a test of our ability to recognize that today's refuse might be tomorrow's resource - the ultimate act of temporal categorization.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">The archaeological record isn't just evidence of past life - it's a fossilized entropy gradient, each layer showing where one generation externalized its disorder so the next could build order on top.</p></div></div><div class="flex flex-col gap-2 py-2"></div></div><div class="absolute bottom-0 right-2 pointer-events-none" style="transform: none;"><div class="rounded-lg transition min-w-max pointer-events-auto translate-x-2 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><button class="inline-flex
