 on registers.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">In a notepad/clipboard model, copy/paste is a syntactic operation on tokens.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">In an LLM-as-clipboard model, the "copy" is actually a predictive summarization — a transformation functor  that rewrites input into a compressed, predictable label.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">So instead of:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">\text{clipboard: } x \mapsto x</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">\text{LLM-clipboard: } x \mapsto F(x) \approx \text{“label of } x\text{”}.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">That means:</p><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Summarization is a functor: it preserves the “morphism structure” of input–output relationships, but compresses/categorizes objects into labels.</li></ol><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Predictability ≈ naturality condition: the LLM clipboard is only useful when its summarization preserves enough structure that applying it repeatedly yields stable labels (idempotence, like normalizing text).</li></ol><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Self-labeling loop:</li></ol><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">A human pastes into the LLM clipboard.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">The model outputs a label (a compressed morphism).</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">That label can itself be copied back in — generating higher-order categories.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">This recursion is effectively the system generating its own ontology.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">In other words: a classical clipboard keeps state unchanged, while an LLM clipboard keeps state predictably transformed. The “copy” isn’t identity, it’s a functorial re-encoding that creates labels for future reference.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">✨ That makes your earlier point sharper: verbal/written numerals are just manipulatives, but LLMs show us that even a transforming clipboard can bootstrap arithmetic and labeling — as long as the functor is predictable enough to stabilize meaning.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Would you like me to formalize this in category-theoretic terms — i.e. show how a predictive summarizer functor can still be used to build the equivalent of Peano arithmetic and self-referential labeling? That might give a rigorous bridge between pebble bags, clipboards, and LLMs.</p></div></div><div class="absolute bottom-0 right-2 pointer-events-none" style="transform: none;"><div class="rounded-lg transition min-w-max pointer-events-auto translate-y-4 bg-bg-100/80 backdrop-blur-sm translate-x-1 group-hover:translate-x-0.5 border-0.5 border-border-300 p-0.5 shadow-sm opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><button class="inline-flex
