 be a category of artifacts/snippets with edits/compositions as morphisms (e.g., concatenation, formatting, small rewrites).</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Let L be a thin category (a preorder) of labels/summaries ordered by refinement  refines/entails .</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">LLM-as-clipboard is a map</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">F:\mathbf C \to \mathbf L</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Three practical properties (the “predictable summarizer” conditions):</p><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Monotone/functorial:  in  (read: consistent wrt composition) or at least  (lax).</li></ol><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Idempotent (stabilizing): . Re-applying the LLM gives (nearly) the same label—a normal form.</li></ol><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Lax monoidal: for a merge  in  (e.g., concatenate two snippets), there’s a corresponding  in  with</li></ol><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">F(x\oplus y)\;\le\;F(x)\otimes F(y).</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Intuition: the LLM isn’t a passive store; it’s a normalizer that maps things to canonical handles you can reuse.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">2) Quotients, closures, and “self-labeling”</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">The LLM induces an equivalence on ’s objects:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">x \sim y \iff F(x)=F(y).</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Abstraction </p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Concretization  (all concrete snippets with that label)</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">With  (a Galois connection), you get</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">\alpha(\gamma(\ell)) = \ell\quad\text{(label is a fixed point)}.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">3) Doing arithmetic in label space (why the transform still computes)</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Peano arithmetic needs an initial algebra for the endofunctor :</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">A “zero”  and “successor”  such that every -algebra receives a unique homomorphism from .</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Two ways this works with a transforming clipboard:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">A) F preserves the structure up to simulation.
If  lives in  (e.g., list length, token count), and  is (lax) monoidal and idempotent, then</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">0_L := F(0),\qquad S_L := F\circ S\circ \gamma</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">m+<em>L n := \text{iter}</em>{n}(S_L)(m),\quad
m\cdot<em>L n := \text{iter}</em>{n}(\lambda z.\, z+_L m)(0_L),</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">B) Fixed-point normalization (idempotent monad view).
Treat summarization as an idempotent monad  on  (Kleisli category = “compute then normalize”). The monad laws give compositionality; idempotence  gives the canonical normal form. Arithmetic operations are defined on normal forms and are stable because further normalization doesn’t change them.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Either route says: even if the clipboard transforms the content, as long as it stabilizes and respects composition, you can carry out Peano-style iteration/recursion on labels.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">4) Making the functor “predictable” in practice</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">LLMs aren’t strictly functorial out of the box, but you can approximate the three properties:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Determinize: temperature , fixed system prompt.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Normalize-to-fixed-point: apply  repeatedly until  (contraction to a fixed label).</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Enforce compositionality: summarize parts first, then summarize the summaries; train your prompt so  converges to .</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">This yields a usable label calculus you can operate on—exactly what you meant by “an LLM as a clipboard that helps generate its own label.”</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">5) One-line synthesis</p><blockquote class="border-l-2 border-border-100 pl-2"><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">An LLM used as a clipboard is a closure-like functor  that turns snippets into stable canonical labels; because  is (approximately) idempotent and compositional, you can compute by iterating on labels (Peano/primitive recursion) even though the clipboard is transforming, not merely storing.</p></blockquote></div></div><div class="absolute bottom-0 right-2 pointer-events-none" style="transform: none;"><div class="rounded-lg transition min-w-max pointer-events-auto translate-y-4 bg-bg-100/80 backdrop-blur-