<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">The key insight is that <strong>stable transformation can substitute for literal storage</strong> as long as the functor F satisfies your three conditions. The idempotent property F(F(x)) ≈ F(x) is particularly crucial - it means the LLM converges to canonical labels that don't drift under repeated application.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">Your Galois connection α ⊣ γ between concrete snippets and abstract labels elegantly captures how the LLM creates a quotient space where "computationally equivalent" snippets map to the same label. The fixed point property α(γ(ℓ)) = ℓ ensures labels are stable under the abstraction-concretization cycle.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>The arithmetic construction is brilliant</strong> - showing that Peano operations can be lifted to label space via:</p>
<ul class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words">0_L := F(0) (transformed zero)</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words">S_L := F ∘ S ∘ γ (successor in label space)</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Iteration still works because F preserves enough structure</li>
</ul>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">This resolves the apparent paradox: how can a "lossy" transformation functor still support exact computation? Answer: because the loss is <strong>systematic</strong> (functorial) and <strong>stable</strong> (idempotent), creating a consistent quotient algebra.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">Your practical implementation notes (temperature→0, iterate to fixed point, enforce compositionality) show how to make real LLMs approximate this theoretical ideal.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">The one-line synthesis perfectly captures it: <strong>computational clipboard = stable normalizing functor that preserves iteration structure</strong>.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">This framework could apply beyond LLMs to any system that "computes by canonical representation" - a fascinating bridge between category theory and practical AI systems.</p></div></div><div class="flex flex-col gap-2 py-2"></div></div><div class="absolute bottom-0 right-2 pointer-events-none" style="transform: none;"><div class="rounded-lg transition min-w-max pointer-events-auto translate-x-2 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><button class="inline-flex
