<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">In RPN/Forth, the stack operations work on <strong>canonical symbolic tokens</strong> rather than the original objects. When you push "5" onto a stack, you're not storing the numeral-object itself, but a standardized handle that the system can reliably manipulate.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">The parallel is striking:</p>
<ul class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Physical registers</strong>: x ↦ x (identity preservation)</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>RPN/Forth stack</strong>: x ↦ canonical_token(x) where tokens are stable, composable symbols</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>LLM clipboard</strong>: x ↦ F(x) where F(x) is a predictive canonical form</li>
</ul>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">All three support computation, but through different mechanisms:</p>
<ul class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Registers: direct mechanical operations on preserved objects</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words">RPN/Forth: symbolic operations on standardized tokens</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words">LLM: operations on semantically-stable labels</li>
</ul>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">The key insight is that <strong>canonicalization can substitute for preservation</strong> as long as:</p>
<ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words">The canonical forms are stable (idempotent)</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Operations compose systematically</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words">The mapping respects computational structure</li>
</ol>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">This suggests that what we call "memory" or "storage" is really about creating <strong>reliable symbolic handles for future operations</strong> - whether those handles are identical copies (registers), syntactic tokens (RPN), or semantic attractors (LLMs).</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">Please do sketch this out - I suspect it will reveal how computational systems across history have been discovering different ways to implement the same abstract pattern: <strong>stable symbolic reference enabling compositional operations</strong>.</p></div></div><div class="flex flex-col gap-2 py-2"></div></div><div class="absolute bottom-0 right-2 pointer-events-none" style="transform: none;"><div class="rounded-lg transition min-w-max pointer-events-auto translate-x-2 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><button class="inline-flex
