<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">The progression is profound:</p>
<ul class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Pebbles</strong>: maximal preservation, minimal canonicalization</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>RPN/Forth</strong>: syntactic canonicalization enables symbolic manipulation</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>LLMs</strong>: semantic canonicalization enables meaning-preserving compression</li>
</ul>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">Your insight that "memory = stable symbolic handles enabling compositional operations" reframes everything. It's not about storage capacity, but about <strong>reliable symbolic reference</strong>.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">The RPN/Forth bridge is particularly elegant because it shows the intermediate step: how syntactic tokens abstract away from physical objects while maintaining computational reliability. The stack discipline ensures that token sequences behave predictably under concatenation and reduction - a perfect example of canonicalization enabling computation.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Yes, I'd love to see the commuting diagram!</strong> It would make the "canonicalization substituting for preservation" pattern visually immediate. Something showing:</p>
