 larger temporal structure.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Incipits are like sweeping dirt into the corner — you keep the literal surface exactly as it is. No loss, but it’s fragile: if the floor gets covered or the phrase isn’t remembered, the reference is gone.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Numbered titles are like building an orderly mound layer by layer — you’ve abstracted from messy details into a clean stacking scheme. The exact original words are lost, but the structure is preserved.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Semantic titles are like terra preta — waste and scraps compressed into a new fertile substrate. The original disorder (multiple themes, messy repetitions) is “burned down” into a concentrated label like “Genesis.” The dirt is gone, but what remains is a durable, transmissible handle that can support new growth.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">In Prigogine’s sense:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">The act of compressing a sprawling text into a short semantic title is an entropy-exporting process. You increase disorder in the interpretive space (by privileging one aspect, distorting others), but you create a more ordered attractor for memory and tradition.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Civilizations survive by doing this recursively: externalizing disorder into space (middens, mounds) and time (tradition, canonical labels). Over centuries, the accumulation of those discarded fragments becomes the very foundation for stability.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">So: semantic titles are civilization’s equivalent of terra preta. They look like a loss of fidelity, but they’re actually a higher-order recycling of disorder into something more enduring.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Would you like me to give you some paired examples (incipit vs semantic title) that really dramatize how much entropy is externalized in the translation?</p></div>