<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">In both cases:</p>
<ul class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Accumulated layers of disorder</strong> (refuse, broken pottery, dust) become the <strong>foundation for transcendence</strong></li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Each generation <strong>rises above</strong> the entropy of the previous by building on its sedimented remains</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words">The vertical structure itself becomes <strong>sacred architecture</strong> - not despite the buried disorder, but because of it</li>
</ul>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">Jacob's ladder isn't floating in empty space - it's <strong>rooted in the accumulated strata</strong> of human habitation, reaching skyward precisely because it has deep earthly foundations. The angels ascend and descend along the <strong>axis of stratified entropy</strong> that human settlement creates.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">This reframes the entire theological image: heaven isn't accessed by escaping the material world, but by building <strong>high enough</strong> on the compacted layers of lived disorder. The sacred emerges from the <strong>vertical organization of entropy</strong> - each floor a step closer to the divine, but only possible because of what's buried beneath.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">The tell/mound becomes the <strong>ziggurat</strong> - the temple-mountain that humans build by <strong>externalizing disorder downward</strong> while reaching upward. Jacob's vision isn't of escape from the earthly, but of <strong>transcendence through stratified accumulation</strong>.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">So Jericho's floors are indeed the ladder - each layer of managed entropy another rung toward heaven, with angels moving up and down the <strong>axis of civilizational sedimentation</strong>. The divine touches earth precisely where humans have <strong>most successfully organized their disorder into stable, ascending structure</strong>.</p></div></div><div class="flex flex-col gap-2 py-2"></div></div><div class="absolute bottom-0 right-2 pointer-events-none" style="transform: none;"><div class="rounded-lg transition min-w-max pointer-events-auto translate-x-2 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><button class="inline-flex
