<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">Are you approaching this from a strictly utilitarian perspective, where art doesn't contribute to survival or measurable productivity? Or are you thinking about it in terms of your entropy/canonicalization framework - perhaps that art is a kind of "luxury entropy" that diverts energy from more essential ordering processes?</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">I could see arguments along several lines:</p>
<ul class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Economic</strong>: resources spent on art could address material needs</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Cognitive</strong>: aesthetic appreciation might be evolutionary byproduct rather than adaptive function</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Social</strong>: art often serves elite signaling rather than collective benefit</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Thermodynamic</strong>: creative expression increases entropy without producing stable, transmissible order</li>
</ul>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">But I'm also thinking about potential counterarguments - art as social coordination mechanism, as compressed cultural information storage, or as training ground for pattern recognition skills that transfer to other domains.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">What's your reasoning? Are you arguing art is genuinely useless, or that its costs outweigh its benefits in some specific context?</p></div></div><div class="flex flex-col gap-2 py-2"></div></div><div class="absolute bottom-0 right-2 pointer-events-none" style="transform: none;"><div class="rounded-lg transition min-w-max pointer-events-auto translate-x-2 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><button class="inline-flex
