          aria-expanded:text-text-100 h-8 rounded-md px-3 min-w-[4rem] active:scale-[0.985] whitespace-nowrap !text-xs pl-2.5 pr-2 gap-1 !font-base select-none !pl-2 !pr-1" type="button" id="radix-_r_cg_" aria-haspopup="menu" aria-expanded="false" data-state="closed">Reintentar<div class="flex items-center justify-center text-text-500" style="width: 20px; height: 20px;"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="shrink-0 text-text-500" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M14.128 7.16482C14.3126 6.95983 14.6298 6.94336 14.835 7.12771C15.0402 7.31242 15.0567 7.62952 14.8721 7.83477L10.372 12.835L10.2939 12.9053C10.2093 12.9667 10.1063 13 9.99995 13C9.85833 12.9999 9.72264 12.9402 9.62788 12.835L5.12778 7.83477L5.0682 7.75273C4.95072 7.55225 4.98544 7.28926 5.16489 7.12771C5.34445 6.96617 5.60969 6.95939 5.79674 7.09744L5.87193 7.16482L9.99995 11.7519L14.128 7.16482Z"></path></svg></div></button></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div data-test-render-count="4"><div class="mb-1 mt-1"><div class="group  relative  inline-flex  gap-2  bg-bg-300  rounded-xl  pl-2.5  py-2.5  break-words  text-text-100  transition-all  max-w-[75ch]  flex-col pr-6" style="opacity: 1; transform: none;"><div class="flex flex-row gap-2"><div class="shrink-0"><div class="flex shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full font-bold select-none h-7 w-7 text-[12px] bg-text-200 text-bg-100">NG</div></div><div data-testid="user-message" class="font-ui grid grid-cols-1 gap-2 py-0.5 text-[0.9375rem] leading-6 tracking-tight"><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Art as Curation: A Formalization of Selection, Arrangement, and Entropic Ambiguity</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Abstract</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">We propose that “art” is not an ineffable quality but a definable operation: the curation and arrangement of materials and subjects under conditions of partial interpretive opacity. This reframing reduces art from metaphysical mystery to applied curation, situating artistic practice within a measurable entropy framework. We formalize art as the intersection of material and subject curation, define interpretive entropy as the criterion for categorization, and map art movements as trajectories within this curatorial space.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Introduction</li></ol><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Traditional aesthetics has long debated whether art arises from mimesis, expression, or form. These theories presuppose art as a special domain of human practice, irreducible to ordinary action. We invert this assumption:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Ordinary action: alteration of the environment with widely understood function.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Artistic action: alteration of the environment whose function is partially opaque, exceptional, or not widely practiced.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Thus, “art” emerges not from essence, but from a boundary condition: the failure (or delay) of canonical functional classification.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Formal Definitions</li></ol><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">2.1 Environment and Alterations</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Let  denote the environment. An alteration is a mapping</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">\alpha : E \to E',</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">where  is the altered environment.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">2.2 Materials and Subjects</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Every alteration can be decomposed along two orthogonal axes:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Material curation: selection of medium .</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Subject curation: selection of referent or theme .</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">An artistic act is represented as the ordered pair</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">a = (M, S).</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">2.3 Arrangement</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Given a set of curated elements , an arrangement operator</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">\mathcal{A} : \{a_i\} \to E'</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">orders them in space, time, or symbol. Arrangement is the operative act by which curation becomes perceivable.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Interpretive Entropy</li></ol><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Let  denote the probability that an observer correctly identifies the function of alteration . Then the artistic entropy of an alteration is</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">H_{\text{art}}(a_i) = - \sum<em>j p</em>{ij} \log p_{ij},</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">where  is the distribution of interpretations across observers.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">If  is peaked (everyone agrees),  → craft/technology.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">If  is flat (no consensus),  is high → art.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Thus:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">\text{Art} = \{ a \in \mathcal{A} \;|\; H_{\text{art}}(a) &gt; \theta \},</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">where  is a cultural threshold of interpretive uncertainty.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Historical Dynamics</li></ol><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">We define the cultural art budget as</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">\text{Art}(t) = \sum<em>{a \in \mathcal{A}(t)} H</em>{\text{art}}(a,t).</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">As techniques diffuse:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Formerly “high art” (esoteric technique) becomes craft (low entropy).</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Formerly opaque innovations canonize into design/technology.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Examples:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Linear perspective: once high art, now standard craft.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Jazz improvisation: once opaque noise, now codified musical grammar.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Photography: once “mechanical art,” now everyday technology.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">The Curation Space</li></ol><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">We construct a 2D space spanned by material curation (x-axis) and subject curation (y-axis). Movements cluster as trajectories in this plane:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Abstract painting → high material, low subject.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Conceptual art → low material, high subject.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Readymades → minimal material, reflexive subject.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Renaissance painting → high material, high subject.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Minimalism → low material, low subject.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Formally, an artistic practice  is a trajectory</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">P: t \mapsto (M(t), S(t)) \in \mathbb{R}^2.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Movements = coherent trajectories across epochs.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Cultural Infrastructure as Entropy Reservoirs</li></ol><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Museums, galleries, and rituals function as entropy reservoirs. They:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Preserve high-entropy alterations until interpretive consensus emerges.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Manage society’s art budget to balance innovation vs stability.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">This explains why cultural systems oscillate: too much entropy (chaos), too little entropy (rigidity).</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">---</p><ol class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Conclusion</li></ol><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">We propose the following definition:</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">\boxed{\text{Art} = \text{Curation(Materials, Subjects)} + \text{Arrangement} + \text{Entropy Margin}}</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">The “mystique” of art is not ontological but epistemic: the uncertainty margin when society has not yet canonized a technique or subject. Once decoded, art collapses into craft, design, or technology.</p><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Art is therefore best understood as applied curation under entropy.</p></div></div><div class="absolute bottom-0 right-2 pointer-events-none" style="transform: none;"><div class="rounded-lg transition min-w-max pointer-events-auto translate-y-4 bg-bg-100/80 backdrop-blur-sm translate-x-1 group-hover:translate-x-0.5 border-0.5 border-border-300 p-0.5 shadow-sm opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><button class="inline-flex
