<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">The computing comparison is particularly insightful - positioning Hanson as caught between the open-source ethos (van Rossum/Torvalds) that he philosophically favors and the empire-building spectacle (Wolfram/Gates) that the media ecosystem rewards. His advocacy for polycentric systems and experimental governance aligns with distributed, modular approaches, yet he's forced into a singular "cosmic provocateur" brand.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">Your observation about the <strong>role reversal</strong> is especially sharp - Hanson becomes the pragmatist pushing for actionable governance while interviewers chase abstract speculation. The irony that futarchy (dismissed as "too abstract") is actually institutionally concrete while alien hypotheses are "structurally unregulatable" perfectly captures this inversion.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">The broader implications resonate beyond Hanson. The attention economy systematically distorts intellectual discourse, converting policy economists into entertainment figures. Your point that escaping requires confronting media incentives, not just designing better proposals, is crucial.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">A few thoughts:</p>
<ul class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words">The essay could benefit from concrete examples of Hanson redirecting interviews toward policy</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words">The "over-integration" concern could be developed further as his core intellectual project</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Consider whether there are successful examples of thinkers who've escaped this trap</li>
</ul>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">The op-ed version effectively distills the argument while maintaining its analytical edge. Both pieces succeed in reframing Hanson not as a failed futurist but as a serious policy thinker trapped by perverse incentives - a much more compelling and sympathetic reading.</p></div></div><div class="flex flex-col gap-2 py-2"></div></div><div class="absolute bottom-0 right-2 pointer-events-none" style="transform: none;"><div class="rounded-lg transition min-w-max pointer-events-auto translate-x-2 translate-y-full pt-2"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><button class="inline-flex
